{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3275","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3275","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3275\",\"parent_revision\":\"41f48e1dbf1c835cb03897cd2acf46bbee04e7bac07fbe783a266b0f1a6b92a1\",\"root_revision\":\"41f48e1dbf1c835cb03897cd2acf46bbee04e7bac07fbe783a266b0f1a6b92a1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-28\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe backlog MCP tools are meant to be provider-neutral \u2014 the configured backend can be GitHub, Beads, SQLite, or Memory (confirmed: `SyncProvider` protocol at `backlog_core/backend_types.py:203` is used throughout `operations.py`, e.g. lines 1168, 1596, 3079, 3739, 3816, 3875, 3925, specifically to gate cache/reconciliation behavior in a backend-agnostic way, cleanly no-oping with \\\"Active backend does not support reconciliation.\\\" for backends that have no cache).\\n\\nDespite that, `backlog_list`'s cache-refresh parameter is named `from_github` (`backlog_core/server.py:1936`), naming one specific backend in a tool meant to work across all of them. The sibling reconcile/pull tool already uses a provider-neutral name for the equivalent concept (`full_refresh`, `server.py:1624`), so the naming is inconsistent even within the existing surface, not just non-neutral in isolation.\\n\\nSeparately, `backlog_view` has no bypass-cache option at all \u2014 its full parameter set (`selector`, `head`, `include_content`, `limit`, `map`, `navigate`, `offset`, `section`, `sections`, `show`, `since`, `skip_tokens`, `summary`) contains nothing equivalent to `from_github`/`full_refresh`. It always reads local cache with no way to ask it to check the live backend first.\\n\\nThis caused a real problem this session: while trying to verify whether recently-groomed content had actually reached GitHub, `backlog_view` could not answer the question \u2014 it would show correctly-synced-looking content regardless of whether anything had actually reached the remote backend, because it never consults the backend directly. Confirming required going outside the tool surface entirely (shelling out to `gh issue view`), and even that took multiple attempts to get right, because it wasn't obvious which of GitHub's own fields corresponds to what this system treats as authoritative.\\n\\nWhat a working fix has to do, stated as outcomes rather than implementation: give every backlog command that reads cached data a consistent way to bypass that cache and validate against the raw backend, named without reference to any specific backend; and make `backlog_view` support it, not just `backlog_list`. A correct implementation must not read a backend's raw storage naively \u2014 for GitHub specifically, the visible issue body is not the authoritative record (see `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` lines 780-789: the issue body is deliberately never overwritten, and the authoritative content lives in an append-only audit-comment record resolved via a head pointer) \u2014 so a live-check option has to go through the same resolution path the existing reconciliation logic already uses, not shortcut around it by reading a backend's most obvious-looking field directly.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 confusion while investigating whether #3169/#3268 grooming had reached GitHub\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"f90208abe6ee4a4a25d54b7f16bca9c8bf55691731323494f9a0dd2c30557c0e\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRRO_Kw\"}"}